</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To benchmark different LLMs with our workflow, we employed both proprietary and open-source models as listed in Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ VI-B LLM Inference Setup ‣ VI Experimental Setup ‣ From Legacy Fortran to Portable Kokkos:An Autonomous Agentic AI Workflow" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> without any LLM fine-tuning. Moreover, this paper focuses on demonstrating the feasibility of using an autonomous agentic workflow using state-of-the-art LLMs. Fine-tuning could be explored in future work to potentially improve performance further, but it is outside the scope of this paper. Proprietary LLMs, such as OpenAI’s models, were accessed via the official OpenAI API. Since these models are usage-based, we had to use tokens for inference and directly account for their costs, which are later reported in our results. Llama 4 Maverick (open-source) was served locally on HPC nodes via Ollama <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> which is a containerized inference engine for LLMs, and the requests to the model were routed through LiteLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, a unified inference proxy providing a consistent API interface. LiteLLM facilitated transparent switching between different model endpoints without changing the underlying workflow logic, thereby simplifying experimentation and deployment. Additionally, we implemented a custom model provider abstraction integrated with the OpenAI Agents SDK. This provider encapsulated logic for dynamic routing based on a runtime configuration, enabling agents to transparently invoke either the OpenAI-hosted models or locally hosted Ollama-served models.</p>
</div>
</section>
